Girls' rights in India, like in any other country, encompass a range of fundamental human rights that are inherent to every individual. It's important to note that these rights are protected by various laws and international conventions. Here are some key aspects of girls' rights in India.

Right to Education: The Right to Education Act in India ensures that every child, including girls, has the right to free and compulsory education up to the age of 14.

Right to Health: Girls have the right to access healthcare services, nutritious food, and a healthy environment. Various government schemes focus on maternal and child health to ensure the well-being of girls and women.

Protection from Discrimination: The Constitution of India prohibits discrimination based on gender. Girls have the right to be treated equally in all aspects of life, including education, employment, and social participation.

Protection from Child Marriage: Laws such as the Prohibition of Child Marriage Act aim to prevent the practice of child marriage, ensuring that girls have the right to choose when and whom to marry.

Right to Expression and Participation: Girls have the right to express their opinions and participate in social, cultural, and political activities. This includes the right to vote and engage in community affairs.

Protection from Violence and Exploitation: Girls have the right to be protected from all forms of violence, abuse, and exploitation. Legal frameworks exist to address crimes like child trafficking, child labor, and sexual exploitation.

Right to a Safe Environment: Every girl has the right to live in a safe and secure environment. Laws against harassment, stalking, and violence aim to protect girls and women from harm.

Right to Inheritance: Girls have the right to inherit property, ensuring economic empowerment and financial independence.

Right to Privacy: The right to privacy is crucial, especially in the age of technology. Girls have the right to be protected from unwarranted intrusion into their private lives.

Right to Freedom of Choice: Girls have the right to make choices about their lives, including education, career, and personal relationships. This encompasses the right to make decisions about their own bodies and reproductive health.

It's important to continue raising awareness about these rights and work towards their effective implementation to ensure that every girl in India can live a life free from discrimination and with access to all opportunities for growth and development.